// Package embervm.node.v1 is the narrow gRPC contract between the EmberVM
// control plane and a node daemon (embervm-noded). It is the clean seam the
// noded fork is shaped by: the daemon owns Firecracker lifecycle behind these
// RPCs, and NOTHING Firecracker-specific leaks across it (no jailer paths, no
// snapshot file paths, no reverse-proxy or session-path semantics inherited
// from the fc-invoke HTTP surface). Refs are opaque strings the control plane
// stores and echoes back; only the daemon interprets them.
//
// The lifecycle this contract expresses, mapped from the forked fc-invoke node
// layer (substrate/node/invoker):
//
//   BuildBase   pull OCI image -> bake rootfs -> cold boot -> health-gate on the
//               guest ready path -> snapshot. Idempotent per (image digest,
//               workload revision). The heavy, once-per-image operation.
//   Prime       restore a pristine VM from a base snapshot, wait ready, park it
//               idle on vsock. The pool-refill primitive; the control plane owns
//               the pool, so this replaces fc-invoke's daemon-side warm base.
//   Assign      deliver exactly one HTTP-semantics task to a primed VM over
//               vsock, return the guest response plus usage stats, then DESTROY
//               the VM regardless of outcome (task-class VMs are single-use, one
//               assignment, no reuse across principals, vsock only / no NIC).
//   Destroy     reap a primed or wedged VM out of band.
//   WatchNode   server-streamed capacity heartbeat; the daemon is the health
//               authority and this is the control plane's only source of node
//               truth.
//   GetNodeStatus  test-only unary snapshot of the same facts. Production
//               consumers use WatchNode.
//
// Concurrency is owned by the control plane, NOT the daemon: the per-workload
// semaphore of fc-invoke is deliberately dropped on the fork. The daemon
// enforces only a node-level max-live-VM cap as a backstop and reports capacity
// facts; the control plane decides what to Prime and Assign.
//
// Auth v1: mTLS is out of scope. The daemon listens on the pod network and uses
// a static bearer token from a Kubernetes Secret, carried in gRPC call metadata
// (key "authorization: Bearer <token>"). This is not network-level caller
// isolation; the removed Cilium policy supplies no defence-in-depth guarantee. The
// upgrade path to mTLS or SPIFFE identities is additive: a new credential field
// on the metadata, not a wire-breaking change to these messages.

